1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,479
Start with a simple question: could life
exist in an

2
00:00:03,479 --> 00:00:06,959
ocean that has never seen the sky? On
Jupiter's

3
00:00:06,959 --> 00:00:10,439
moon Europa, a global saltwater ocean lies
hidden beneath a

4
00:00:10,439 --> 00:00:13,919
completely frozen crust. That crust is
tens of miles

5
00:00:13,919 --> 00:00:17,399
thick, and the ocean surface beneath it is
frozen solid.

6
00:00:17,399 --> 00:00:20,662
Yet the ocean underneath holds more than
twice as

7
00:00:20,662 --> 00:00:23,924
much water as all of Earth's oceans
combined. It

8
00:00:23,924 --> 00:00:27,187
stays liquid not because of sunlight, but
because Jupiter's

9
00:00:27,187 --> 00:00:30,449
gravity flexes the moon and heats it from
within.

10
00:00:30,449 --> 00:00:33,932
Sunlight never reaches that water, so
nothing down there has

11
00:00:33,932 --> 00:00:37,414
ever seen a sunrise. So the question
becomes sharper:

12
00:00:37,414 --> 00:00:40,896
what could live in a dark ocean sealed
under ice?

13
00:00:40,896 --> 00:00:44,378
Europa is not even the only one: Saturn's
moon

14
00:00:44,378 --> 00:00:47,859
Enceladus hides a global salty ocean
beneath its icy crust.

15
00:00:47,859 --> 00:00:51,859
Enceladus even sprays that ocean into
space through cracks near

16
00:00:51,859 --> 00:00:55,859
its south pole. On Earth, we already know
what life can

17
00:00:55,859 --> 00:00:59,859
do without sunlight, because the deep sea
has an answer.

18
00:00:59,859 --> 00:01:03,575
This episode follows that answer outward,
from a vent on

19
00:01:03,575 --> 00:01:07,290
our own sea floor to an ocean we have
never

20
00:01:07,289 --> 00:01:11,004
touched. Some of what follows is measured,
and some of it

21
00:01:11,004 --> 00:01:14,719
is invented, and we will mark the
difference every time.

22
00:01:14,719 --> 00:01:18,719
By the end, you can decide which parts

23
00:01:18,719 --> 00:01:22,719
you believe. Two of the least
remarkable-looking moons in

24
00:01:22,719 --> 00:01:26,719
the solar system may be the most
interesting.

25
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,707
Begin with the shape of the world: an
ocean with no

26
00:00:03,707 --> 00:00:07,415
shore you could walk out onto, capped by
ice. Europa's ocean

27
00:00:07,415 --> 00:00:11,122
is global, which means it wraps the entire
moon. There is

28
00:00:11,122 --> 00:00:14,830
no beach here, no coastline, no place
where water meets air.

29
00:00:14,830 --> 00:00:17,998
Above the water sits a crust of ice tens

30
00:00:17,998 --> 00:00:21,166
of miles thick. That ice stays frozen
where it meets

31
00:00:21,166 --> 00:00:24,333
the water, so the boundary is a lid, not

32
00:00:24,334 --> 00:00:27,502
a shoreline. The water below is the
striking part: more

33
00:00:27,502 --> 00:00:30,670
than twice the volume of every ocean on
Earth.

34
00:00:30,670 --> 00:00:34,375
Twice over, in a single moon. Volume is
not the same

35
00:00:34,375 --> 00:00:38,079
as habitability, but it does buy room.
Room for chemistry, room

36
00:00:38,079 --> 00:00:41,784
for gradients, room for time. The heat
that keeps it liquid

37
00:00:41,785 --> 00:00:45,490
comes from Jupiter, which pulls on Europa
as the moon orbits.

38
00:00:45,490 --> 00:00:48,792
That flexing bends the moon, and the
bending becomes heat.

39
00:00:48,792 --> 00:00:52,094
Tidal heating is why the ocean is not a
block

40
00:00:52,094 --> 00:00:55,396
of ice all the way through. It also drives

41
00:00:55,396 --> 00:00:58,698
chemistry between the ocean and the rocky
floor beneath it.

42
00:00:58,698 --> 00:01:02,000
Enceladus, at Saturn, is smaller but
follows the same logic.

43
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:05,202
It too carries a global salty ocean under
an icy

44
00:01:05,202 --> 00:01:08,405
crust. Two moons, two oceans, both sealed
away from

45
00:01:08,405 --> 00:01:11,607
the sky. If you wanted to design a place

46
00:01:11,607 --> 00:01:14,810
where life could hide, you would struggle
to do better.

47
00:01:14,810 --> 00:01:18,248
But hiding is not the same as living, and
for

48
00:01:18,248 --> 00:01:21,686
that a world needs an energy source. So
the next

49
00:01:21,686 --> 00:01:25,124
thing to measure is not the size of the
ocean, but

50
00:01:25,123 --> 00:01:28,561
what powers it. Keep the image in your
head: an

51
00:01:28,561 --> 00:01:32,000
ocean with no shores, roofed by ice,
warmed from below.

52
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:35,537
An ocean with more water than ours, and no
way

53
00:01:35,537 --> 00:01:39,074
for the sun to reach it. Nothing about
that lid is

54
00:01:39,075 --> 00:01:42,612
thin enough to see through. If you poured
Earth's oceans into

55
00:01:42,612 --> 00:01:46,150
it, you would still have room for another
Earth's worth.

56
00:01:46,150 --> 00:01:49,520
The moon is not a dead ball of ice, it

57
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:52,890
is being worked on from the outside, every
orbit. That

58
00:01:52,890 --> 00:01:56,260
is the first surprise of this world: the
heat comes

59
00:01:56,260 --> 00:01:59,630
from a neighbour, not from the star.
Enceladus is smaller,

60
00:01:59,629 --> 00:02:03,000
but the same three layers appear: ice,
water, and rock.

61
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:07,000
Does a smaller world keep its

62
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,000
ocean as easily? That is a question

63
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:15,000
only the mission data can answer.

64
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,745
Here is the problem the ice creates: no
sunlight reaches

65
00:00:03,745 --> 00:00:07,490
the water, so photosynthesis is off the
table. On

66
00:00:07,490 --> 00:00:11,234
Earth, life solved that problem in the
dark, at

67
00:00:11,234 --> 00:00:14,980
hydrothermal vents. Vent ecosystems run on
chemosynthesis instead of sunlight.

68
00:00:14,980 --> 00:00:19,053
Chemosynthetic bacteria take chemical
energy from hydrogen sulfide and carbon

69
00:00:19,053 --> 00:00:23,126
dioxide and turn inorganic chemicals into
food. That makes them the

70
00:00:23,126 --> 00:00:27,199
base of the food web, not the top of it.

71
00:00:27,199 --> 00:00:30,667
Around those bacteria live vent shrimp,
tubeworms and mussels,

72
00:00:30,667 --> 00:00:34,135
none of which need a single photon. So the

73
00:00:34,135 --> 00:00:37,603
template exists, and it is not
hypothetical on

74
00:00:37,603 --> 00:00:41,072
Earth. Enceladus appears to have the same
kind of

75
00:00:41,072 --> 00:00:44,539
machinery: vents where its ocean meets a
rocky core.

76
00:00:44,539 --> 00:00:47,771
Cassini found silica grains in the plume
that

77
00:00:47,771 --> 00:00:51,003
formed where liquid water and rock
interacted above

78
00:00:51,003 --> 00:00:54,235
ninety degrees Celsius. That is hot rock
in contact

79
00:00:54,236 --> 00:00:57,468
with water, under an ice shell, far from

80
00:00:57,468 --> 00:01:00,700
the sun. The plume also carries molecular
hydrogen.

81
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:04,168
Microbes could pair that hydrogen with
dissolved carbon dioxide

82
00:01:04,168 --> 00:01:07,636
and harvest the reaction as energy. Read
that again:

83
00:01:07,636 --> 00:01:11,104
a chemical battery, vented into space,
from an

84
00:01:11,104 --> 00:01:14,572
ocean nobody has visited. At Europa, tidal
heating supplies

85
00:01:14,572 --> 00:01:18,040
the energy and drives chemistry at the sea
floor.

86
00:01:18,040 --> 00:01:21,430
Two different moons, two routes to the
same three

87
00:01:21,430 --> 00:01:24,820
ingredients: water, chemistry, energy.
That combination is why

88
00:01:24,820 --> 00:01:28,210
these moons keep appearing on lists of
places to

89
00:01:28,210 --> 00:01:31,600
look for life. It is also why the

90
00:01:31,599 --> 00:01:34,989
next part of this episode has to be
careful.

91
00:01:34,989 --> 00:01:38,637
Because the ingredients are measured, but
the inhabitants are

92
00:01:38,637 --> 00:01:42,284
not. Hydrogen and carbon dioxide are a
possible energy

93
00:01:42,284 --> 00:01:45,932
source, not proof that anything is using
them. Possible is

94
00:01:45,932 --> 00:01:49,579
the honest word, and it is worth holding
onto.

95
00:01:49,579 --> 00:01:53,127
With the chemistry established, the
question moves from what

96
00:01:53,127 --> 00:01:56,674
a world provides to what a body could do

97
00:01:56,674 --> 00:02:00,222
with it. No sunlight, no photosynthesis,
and still a

98
00:02:00,222 --> 00:02:03,769
full ecosystem: that is what the vents
show us.

99
00:02:03,769 --> 00:02:07,317
Water plus hot rock leaves mineral traces,
and the

100
00:02:07,317 --> 00:02:10,865
plume carries those traces outward.
Hydrogen is the part

101
00:02:10,865 --> 00:02:14,413
that matters for biology, because it can
power a

102
00:02:14,413 --> 00:02:17,961
microbe without any light. Notice what is
missing from

103
00:02:17,961 --> 00:02:21,509
that list: sunshine, plants, and anything
with a face.

104
00:02:21,509 --> 00:02:25,509
At Europa the heat arrives through

105
00:02:25,509 --> 00:02:29,509
the ice, from a parent planet,

106
00:02:29,509 --> 00:02:33,509
and reaches the water from below.

107
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,222
From here on, we stop describing and start
designing.

108
00:00:03,222 --> 00:00:06,445
Everything from this point is invented:
honestly labelled, constrained

109
00:00:06,445 --> 00:00:09,667
by physics, and never observed. The rule
we set ourselves

110
00:00:09,667 --> 00:00:12,890
was simple: no creature without a reason
to exist.

111
00:00:12,890 --> 00:00:16,635
Every body plan has to answer three
questions: what does it

112
00:00:16,635 --> 00:00:20,380
eat, what is trying to eat it, and how
does it

113
00:00:20,380 --> 00:00:24,125
move. Start where the energy is: the
bacterial mats that

114
00:00:24,125 --> 00:00:27,870
coat vent chimneys. The vent grazer is
built to harvest them.

115
00:00:27,870 --> 00:00:31,265
It has six limbs, and it uses them to grip

116
00:00:31,265 --> 00:00:34,660
the chimney while it grazes. Six points of
contact hold

117
00:00:34,660 --> 00:00:38,055
it steady in the rising vent flow. Its
body

118
00:00:38,055 --> 00:00:41,450
is low and broad, a shape that suits
crushing pressure.

119
00:00:41,450 --> 00:00:44,672
We gave it a long neck, so it can

120
00:00:44,672 --> 00:00:47,894
reach mats on the chimney wall without
pressing its

121
00:00:47,895 --> 00:00:51,117
body into hot fluid. That is a design
choice, not

122
00:00:51,117 --> 00:00:54,340
an observation, and it is written down as
one.

123
00:00:54,340 --> 00:00:57,650
The filter drifter takes a different route
to the same

124
00:00:57,650 --> 00:01:00,960
food: it filters microbes out of the
water. It

125
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:04,269
swims with a single undulating fin. No
limbs, one

126
00:01:04,269 --> 00:01:07,579
continuous fin, and a wide mouth that
strains the water.

127
00:01:07,579 --> 00:01:11,061
We kept it limbless on purpose, because
limbs are expensive

128
00:01:11,061 --> 00:01:14,543
when you never need to grip anything. We
also kept

129
00:01:14,543 --> 00:01:18,025
both animals small in frame, because an
invented creature should

130
00:01:18,025 --> 00:01:21,507
not be sold as a portrait. Now put them in

131
00:01:21,507 --> 00:01:24,989
the same water and the scene stops being a
catalogue.

132
00:01:24,989 --> 00:01:28,734
Mats grow where vent fluid meets cold
water, and drifters gather

133
00:01:28,734 --> 00:01:32,480
where mats break apart. One grazes the
surface of the rock,

134
00:01:32,479 --> 00:01:36,224
and one harvests what the current carries
away. That is

135
00:01:36,224 --> 00:01:39,969
a food web in miniature, and it is
entirely our invention.

136
00:01:39,969 --> 00:01:43,173
Which raises the question this episode
cannot answer with

137
00:01:43,173 --> 00:01:46,377
data: what would eat them? The drifter's
body is

138
00:01:46,378 --> 00:01:49,581
a ribbon, flattened and wide, a shape that
does not

139
00:01:49,581 --> 00:01:52,785
fight the water. Neither animal has eyes
in our

140
00:01:52,786 --> 00:01:55,989
design, because there is nothing down here
to see.

141
00:01:55,989 --> 00:01:59,212
The grazer's food is a mat, and a mat

142
00:01:59,212 --> 00:02:02,434
is a surface, so the animal has to hold

143
00:02:02,435 --> 00:02:05,657
position against a current. Two
strategies, one pantry: one animal

144
00:02:05,657 --> 00:02:08,879
farms the rock, and the other skims the
water.

145
00:02:08,879 --> 00:02:12,879
If you have ever wondered what

146
00:02:12,879 --> 00:02:16,879
a food web looks like before it

147
00:02:16,879 --> 00:02:20,879
has a top, this is it.

148
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,420
Here we have to be honest: there is no
evidence

149
00:00:03,420 --> 00:00:06,840
for an alien predator, because nobody has
seen one.

150
00:00:06,840 --> 00:00:10,260
What we can do is reason: if a mat feeds

151
00:00:10,260 --> 00:00:13,680
a grazer, the same energy is available to
something

152
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:17,100
that eats grazers. That is not evidence,
it is bookkeeping.

153
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:20,304
Energy that collects in one body becomes a
target

154
00:00:20,304 --> 00:00:23,509
for another. So a plausible ocean would
not stop

155
00:00:23,510 --> 00:00:26,715
at grazers. A hunter here would face the
same

156
00:00:26,715 --> 00:00:29,920
constraints as everything else: pressure,
cold, and permanent dark.

157
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:33,484
We did not design one, because a predator
is where

158
00:00:33,484 --> 00:00:37,048
invention starts to outrun constraint.
Instead, ask what an arms

159
00:00:37,048 --> 00:00:40,612
race would even look like without light.
One answer is

160
00:00:40,612 --> 00:00:44,176
already in our design set: light. The vent
grazer signals

161
00:00:44,176 --> 00:00:47,740
with bioluminescence, in an ocean that
never sees the sun.

162
00:00:47,740 --> 00:00:51,035
In permanent darkness, light is the only
language that

163
00:00:51,035 --> 00:00:54,330
travels. But a signal that travels is also
a

164
00:00:54,329 --> 00:00:57,625
signal that can be intercepted. That is
the whole logic

165
00:00:57,625 --> 00:01:00,920
of an arms race: every advantage becomes a
vulnerability.

166
00:01:00,920 --> 00:01:04,340
Camouflage against a glow, speed against a
slow grazer, a

167
00:01:04,340 --> 00:01:07,760
harder shell against a jaw. Each of those
is

168
00:01:07,760 --> 00:01:11,180
a guess about a guess, and we are not
going

169
00:01:11,180 --> 00:01:14,600
to dress them up as findings. What we can

170
00:01:14,599 --> 00:01:18,019
say is that competition would not require
sunlight, only scarcity.

171
00:01:18,019 --> 00:01:21,225
Bacterial mats coat the chimneys, which
makes the food

172
00:01:21,225 --> 00:01:24,432
patchy by construction. Patchy food makes
territory, and territory

173
00:01:24,432 --> 00:01:27,638
makes conflict. None of this is observed:
all of

174
00:01:27,638 --> 00:01:30,844
it is the reasoning a field biologist
would use

175
00:01:30,843 --> 00:01:34,049
on Earth, pointed at a place we cannot
visit.

176
00:01:34,049 --> 00:01:37,611
And that is the limit of this section: the
shape

177
00:01:37,611 --> 00:01:41,173
of an argument, with no animal attached to
it. So

178
00:01:41,173 --> 00:01:44,735
we leave the predators unbuilt, and say so
out loud.

179
00:01:44,735 --> 00:01:48,297
A predator is not a monster in this story,
it

180
00:01:48,298 --> 00:01:51,859
is a bookkeeping entry that has not been
filled in.

181
00:01:51,859 --> 00:01:55,509
And an unfilled entry is more honest than
a creature

182
00:01:55,509 --> 00:01:59,159
invented to fill it. Our grazer already
carries a light,

183
00:01:59,159 --> 00:02:02,810
which means it already carries a risk.
Every signal in the

184
00:02:02,810 --> 00:02:06,459
dark is a decision about who gets to find
you.

185
00:02:06,459 --> 00:02:10,460
That is the arms race in

186
00:02:10,460 --> 00:02:14,460
one sentence, and we can leave

187
00:02:14,460 --> 00:02:18,460
it there without inventing a winner.

188
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,189
Now go down, past the mats, to the floor

189
00:00:03,189 --> 00:00:06,379
of the world. At Enceladus, that floor is
where

190
00:00:06,379 --> 00:00:09,570
the ocean meets rock, and where the vents

191
00:00:09,570 --> 00:00:12,759
are. The silica grains in the plume are
the

192
00:00:12,759 --> 00:00:15,949
receipt: water and hot rock, interacting
above ninety degrees.

193
00:00:15,949 --> 00:00:19,394
At Europa, the same meeting point would be
the sea

194
00:00:19,394 --> 00:00:22,839
floor, under an ocean twice the size of
ours.

195
00:00:22,839 --> 00:00:26,285
The ice burrower lives in the sediment at
that

196
00:00:26,285 --> 00:00:29,730
boundary. It moves by peristalsis, pushing
through sediment under load.

197
00:00:29,730 --> 00:00:33,354
Near-freezing water and high pressure are
the two constraints its

198
00:00:33,354 --> 00:00:36,979
body answers. We gave it no limbs, because
limbs do

199
00:00:36,979 --> 00:00:40,604
not help in mud. It does not swim and it

200
00:00:40,604 --> 00:00:44,229
does not graze: it simply moves through
the ground, slowly.

201
00:00:44,229 --> 00:00:47,672
Everything down here ultimately traces
back to chemosynthesis, the same

202
00:00:47,672 --> 00:00:51,115
chemistry as Earth's vents. The grazer
stays near the

203
00:00:51,114 --> 00:00:54,557
chimneys, where the mats are. And it
reproduces by

204
00:00:54,557 --> 00:00:58,000
budding, at the vents, where chemical
energy is most abundant.

205
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:01,897
Budding is a design choice: no mate
required, no season required,

206
00:01:01,897 --> 00:01:05,795
just energy and time. In a world sealed
under ice, there

207
00:01:05,795 --> 00:01:09,692
is no daylight to synchronise with. So
reproduction would have

208
00:01:09,692 --> 00:01:13,590
to be tied to something else: chemistry,
temperature, or each other.

209
00:01:13,590 --> 00:01:17,215
Communication, in that world, would have
to travel without any

210
00:01:17,215 --> 00:01:20,840
light from above. Bioluminescence is our
answer: the creature makes

211
00:01:20,840 --> 00:01:24,465
its own light. It is also the least
certain thing

212
00:01:24,465 --> 00:01:28,090
in this episode, and we are flagging it as
such.

213
00:01:28,090 --> 00:01:31,532
Three invented animals, one shared
constraint: everything must work in

214
00:01:31,532 --> 00:01:34,974
the dark, under pressure, at near-freezing
temperature. The grazer

215
00:01:34,974 --> 00:01:38,417
grips, the drifter filters, the burrower
pushes. None of

216
00:01:38,417 --> 00:01:41,859
them has been seen, and all of them are
possible.

217
00:01:41,859 --> 00:01:45,414
Sediment is the quietest habitat in this
world, and the

218
00:01:45,414 --> 00:01:48,968
burrower is built for quiet. Budding means
the young are

219
00:01:48,968 --> 00:01:52,522
not scattered, they start where the energy
already is.

220
00:01:52,521 --> 00:01:56,075
The chimney is a tower of chemistry, and
the grazer

221
00:01:56,075 --> 00:01:59,629
is the animal that learned to hold on to
it.

222
00:01:59,629 --> 00:02:03,626
Three animals, three answers to the same
exam question: how do

223
00:02:03,626 --> 00:02:07,623
you make a living where nothing grows from
light? On Earth,

224
00:02:07,623 --> 00:02:11,620
that exam has already been passed, in
water just as dark.

225
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:15,620
And that is why the deep

226
00:02:15,620 --> 00:02:19,620
is the closest thing we

227
00:02:19,620 --> 00:02:23,620
have to a rehearsal for Europa.

228
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,457
Suppose life did appear under that ice:
would it look

229
00:00:03,457 --> 00:00:06,915
like our designs, or like nothing we could
guess? This

230
00:00:06,915 --> 00:00:10,372
is the part of the episode where the
honest answer is

231
00:00:10,372 --> 00:00:13,830
that we do not know, and neither does
anyone else.

232
00:00:13,830 --> 00:00:17,457
There are two ways to think about it, and
they point

233
00:00:17,457 --> 00:00:21,085
in opposite directions. The first says
that physics narrows the options:

234
00:00:21,085 --> 00:00:24,712
if you need to grip rock in a current, six

235
00:00:24,712 --> 00:00:28,339
points of contact is one of a small number
of answers.

236
00:00:28,339 --> 00:00:32,219
The second says that history matters more
than physics: what survives depends

237
00:00:32,219 --> 00:00:36,100
on what happened to arrive first. Under
that view, our grazer

238
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:39,980
is not a prediction, it is a coincidence
waiting to be

239
00:00:39,980 --> 00:00:43,859
disproved. Both views are respectable, and
neither is testable from a desk.

240
00:00:43,859 --> 00:00:47,369
That is why the mission matters more than
the argument.

241
00:00:47,369 --> 00:00:50,879
Europa Clipper is being built to measure
the ice shell, the

242
00:00:50,879 --> 00:00:54,389
ocean, and the surface chemistry. Future
missions could sample Enceladus'

243
00:00:54,390 --> 00:00:57,899
plumes directly, looking for the chemistry
of life. Until then, every

244
00:00:57,899 --> 00:01:01,409
creature in this episode is a hypothesis
with a shape.

245
00:01:01,409 --> 00:01:04,952
And a hypothesis with a shape is easier to
argue

246
00:01:04,952 --> 00:01:08,495
with than a vague hope. That is the point
of designing

247
00:01:08,495 --> 00:01:12,037
them at all: to make our assumptions
visible. If the ocean

248
00:01:12,037 --> 00:01:15,579
is empty, our designs are wrong in an
interesting way.

249
00:01:15,579 --> 00:01:19,039
And if it is not empty, they are probably
wrong

250
00:01:19,039 --> 00:01:22,500
in a more interesting way. Either outcome
teaches us something,

251
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:25,960
and that is the best a guess can do. So
would

252
00:01:25,960 --> 00:01:29,420
this world repeat? We will not know until
someone looks.

253
00:01:29,420 --> 00:01:33,420
The two views do not cancel each other,
they set

254
00:01:33,420 --> 00:01:37,420
the size of the bet. Either way, the ocean
is the

255
00:01:37,420 --> 00:01:41,420
thing to look at, and the ice is the door.

256
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,547
There is one more question, and it is the
strangest

257
00:00:03,547 --> 00:00:07,094
one in the episode. If this ocean had
life, could it

258
00:00:07,094 --> 00:00:10,642
ever build something? Not a civilisation,
something simpler: a tool, a

259
00:00:10,642 --> 00:00:14,189
structure, a signal that carries further
than a body can.

260
00:00:14,189 --> 00:00:18,190
Start with the obvious obstacle: there is
no dry land to

261
00:00:18,190 --> 00:00:22,190
stand on. Everything happens in water, and
water is a medium

262
00:00:22,190 --> 00:00:26,190
that pushes back. The second obstacle is
heat you can hold.

263
00:00:26,190 --> 00:00:29,570
A vent gives heat, but it gives it at the

264
00:00:29,570 --> 00:00:32,950
bottom of an ocean, in a place you cannot
carry

265
00:00:32,950 --> 00:00:36,330
it away from. The third obstacle is
material: our own

266
00:00:36,329 --> 00:00:39,710
technology leans on solids that can be
shaped and kept.

267
00:00:39,710 --> 00:00:43,710
Rock and ice exist here, but whether they
can be worked

268
00:00:43,710 --> 00:00:47,710
is a different question. And if a vent
field shifts

269
00:00:47,710 --> 00:00:51,710
or cools, anything built on it has to move
with it.

270
00:00:51,710 --> 00:00:55,292
So a technological species here would need
a body that works

271
00:00:55,292 --> 00:00:58,874
in water, hands or their equivalent, and a
reason to

272
00:00:58,874 --> 00:01:02,456
build at all. That is a long list of
conditions, and

273
00:01:02,456 --> 00:01:06,038
each one is a place where the story could
stop.

274
00:01:06,038 --> 00:01:09,620
It is also the reason this section is
short on conclusions.

275
00:01:09,620 --> 00:01:13,082
We could invent a tool-maker, but
inventing one would tell

276
00:01:13,082 --> 00:01:16,545
you nothing about this ocean. Nothing we
have measured rules

277
00:01:16,545 --> 00:01:20,007
intelligence in or out. That is an honest
place to leave

278
00:01:20,007 --> 00:01:23,469
it: an open question with no evidence on
either side.

279
00:01:23,469 --> 00:01:27,019
And it is worth saying plainly that the
absence of

280
00:01:27,019 --> 00:01:30,569
evidence is not a verdict. And what
happens to a tool

281
00:01:30,569 --> 00:01:34,119
left in salt water for a century? Every
obstacle here is

282
00:01:34,120 --> 00:01:37,670
a place where a different world would have
an advantage.

283
00:01:37,670 --> 00:01:41,670
Which is why the honest answer

284
00:01:41,670 --> 00:01:45,670
to the technology question is

285
00:01:45,670 --> 00:01:49,670
not no, but not yet measurable.

286
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,339
So let us separate the two piles, starting
with

287
00:00:03,339 --> 00:00:06,679
what is measured. Europa has a global
saltwater ocean

288
00:00:06,679 --> 00:00:10,019
under a completely frozen crust. That
ocean holds

289
00:00:10,019 --> 00:00:13,359
more than twice the water of all Earth's
oceans.

290
00:00:13,359 --> 00:00:16,795
Jupiter's tidal heating keeps it liquid
and drives chemistry

291
00:00:16,795 --> 00:00:20,230
at the sea floor. Enceladus has a global
salty

292
00:00:20,230 --> 00:00:23,664
ocean under ice. Its vents put hot rock in

293
00:00:23,664 --> 00:00:27,100
contact with water, and the plume carries
the evidence.

294
00:00:27,100 --> 00:00:30,630
Its plume also carries hydrogen, a
chemical energy source

295
00:00:30,630 --> 00:00:34,160
microbes could use. And on Earth, vent
life runs

296
00:00:34,159 --> 00:00:37,689
on chemosynthesis, without sunlight.
Everything else is ours: the grazer,

297
00:00:37,689 --> 00:00:41,219
the drifter, the burrower, the budding,
and the glow.

298
00:00:41,219 --> 00:00:44,462
Five inventions, each with a stated
constraint, none

299
00:00:44,462 --> 00:00:47,704
with an observation. That is the whole
list, and

300
00:00:47,704 --> 00:00:50,947
it is short on purpose. A longer list
would

301
00:00:50,947 --> 00:00:54,189
have meant weaker sourcing, not a better
episode.

302
00:00:54,189 --> 00:00:57,435
If you remember one thing, make it this:

303
00:00:57,435 --> 00:01:00,680
the ocean is real, and the animals are
not.

304
00:01:00,679 --> 00:01:03,924
Enceladus supplies the strongest chemical
case, because its ocean

305
00:01:03,924 --> 00:01:07,170
is sampled for us, by its own plumes.

306
00:01:07,170 --> 00:01:10,450
Notice the asymmetry: eight claims about
the world, and

307
00:01:10,450 --> 00:01:13,730
five about animals we made up. The Earth

308
00:01:13,730 --> 00:01:17,010
analogue is the reason any of this is
worth

309
00:01:17,009 --> 00:01:20,289
arguing about. If the vents had no life,

310
00:01:20,289 --> 00:01:23,569
this episode would be science fiction with
better footnotes.

311
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,476
A frozen alien ocean may be the best place
in

312
00:00:03,476 --> 00:00:06,952
the solar system to look for life. Not
because we have

313
00:00:06,952 --> 00:00:10,428
found it, but because the physics allows
it. Water, chemistry,

314
00:00:10,428 --> 00:00:13,903
energy: the ingredients are there. The
rest is a question for

315
00:00:13,903 --> 00:00:17,379
the next mission, and for the people who
fly it.

316
00:00:17,379 --> 00:00:20,855
So here is our question for you. If you
had

317
00:00:20,855 --> 00:00:24,331
one instrument to send under that ice,
what would you measure

318
00:00:24,332 --> 00:00:27,807
first? The chemistry, the temperature, or
the sound of something

319
00:00:27,807 --> 00:00:31,283
moving? Tell us in the comments, and tell
us why. Because

320
00:00:31,283 --> 00:00:34,759
that is how a guess becomes a question
worth funding.

321
00:00:34,759 --> 00:00:38,019
It is a moon of Jupiter, and it has been

322
00:00:38,019 --> 00:00:41,279
there the whole time. The ocean is waiting
under the

323
00:00:41,280 --> 00:00:44,539
ice, and it is not going anywhere. Until
then,

324
00:00:44,539 --> 00:00:47,799
keep asking the question, and keep asking
for the evidence.

